James <james.kanze@gmail.com> wrote:
>C++ would definitely be an improvement over C.  If you are only
>targetting a closed set of compilers and systems, it would be my
>first choice.  But portability still remains a serious problem
>with C++; code which one compiler accepts will often be rejected
>by another.

If one compiler accepts a piece of code and another rejects it, then one
or more of the following apply:
 - Your code doesn't conform to the C++ standard.
 - The accepting compiler doesn't conform to the C++ standard.
 - The rejecting compiler doesn't conform to the C++ standard.

The GNU C++ compiler is free, can target just about any platform that
can accommodate Nethack 3.4.3 (yes, including Windows), and has a
significant number of standards-adherence martinets on the development
team.

>I'd rather not see such hackers getting involved.  The main
>goal, I thought, was to end up with a clean code base, and not a
>bowl of spaghetti.

If you want a clean code base, the only thing you should use the Nethack
source code for is an empirical test case. "Does this constructed
situation do the same thing in our built-from-scratch game as it does in
Nethack? If not, do we care?"

At that point, of course, you should use the Language You Know Best.
